N,R,S="N","R","S"
SCENES={
"title":[(N,"Operation Gate. The Vault of Tables. Databases and data warehousing for GATE Data Science and A I, twenty twenty seven."),
(N,"Seven vaults, seven twists, real Python and real S Q L running in every vault.")],
"er":[(N,"Vault one. E R and relational models."),
(R,"Why do we draw boxes and diamonds before we make tables?"),
(S,"Because a database is a model of the real world, and mistakes are cheapest on paper. An entity is a thing, like a student. Attributes describe it. A relationship connects entities, like a student enrols in a course."),
(S,"Cardinality says how many. One to one, one to many, many to many. One department has many employees. But one student takes many courses, and one course has many students."),
(R,"How does a diamond become a table?"),
(S,"Each entity becomes a table. A one to many relationship puts the key of the one side into the many side, as a foreign key. A many to many relationship becomes a new table of its own."),
(S,"Keys. A superkey identifies a row. A candidate key is a smallest superkey. The primary key is the candidate you chose. A foreign key points to a primary key somewhere else."),
(N,"Twist one. A many to many relationship cannot live in two tables. It needs a third table, Enrolment, whose key is the pair, student and course. That one table is the heart of most E R questions.")],
"sql":[(N,"Vault two. S Q L."),
(R,"Why does S Q L look so different from a program?"),
(S,"Because you say what you want, not how to get it. The order you write is select, from, where, group by, having, order by. But the database runs from, where, group by, having, select."),
(S,"Take five employees. Group by department, and keep only groups with more than one row. Look at the code on the left, and the real answer it printed."),
(R,"Why does E E show two rows but one salary?"),
(S,"Because count star counts rows, count of salary skips the missing value, and the average also skips it. So E E has two rows, one salary, and an average of sixty."),
(S,"Joins. An inner join keeps matching pairs only. A left join keeps every left row and fills the missing side with NULL. A subquery is a query inside a query, used in where or from."),
(S,"Now the trap. Where salary equals NULL returns zero rows, even though one salary is missing. Only IS NULL finds it."),
(N,"Twist two. NULL is not a value, it is unknown. Anything compared with unknown is unknown, and where keeps only true. Where versus having is the other classic. Where filters rows before grouping, having filters groups after.")],
"ic":[(N,"Vault three. Integrity constraints."),
(R,"Why not just trust the program to put in correct data?"),
(S,"Because programs have bugs and people have typos. A constraint is a lock inside the database. Not null means the value must exist. Unique means no duplicates. Primary key is both. Check means a condition must hold."),
(S,"Referential integrity is the foreign key rule. Every foreign key value must match an existing primary key, or be NULL. Look at the code. We try to add an employee in department ninety nine, which does not exist."),
(S,"The database refuses, with foreign key constraint failed. Then we delete the department. Because of on delete cascade, the employee disappears with it."),
(R,"What are the other choices on delete?"),
(S,"Restrict, which blocks the delete. Set null, which blanks the child. And cascade, which deletes the children. Choose by asking what the child means without its parent."),
(N,"Twist three. A unique column may still contain several NULLs, because unknown is never equal to unknown. But a primary key can never be NULL.")],
"norm":[(N,"Vault four. Normalization."),
(R,"What is wrong with one big table?"),
(S,"Redundancy, and three anomalies. If a department's phone is stored in every employee row, changing it means many updates. Deleting the last employee loses the department. Adding a department with no employees is impossible."),
(S,"The cure is functional dependencies. X determines Y means equal X always gives equal Y. The closure of X is everything X can determine. Look at the code. With A gives B, B gives C, C D gives E, the closure of A is A, B, C."),
(S,"The closure of A D is all five attributes, so A D is a key. Closure is a loop that keeps adding until nothing changes. Every key question in the exam is this loop."),
(R,"And the normal forms?"),
(S,"First normal form, every cell is one value. Second, no partial dependency on part of a key. Third, every non key attribute depends on the key, the whole key and nothing but the key. B C N F, every determinant is a superkey."),
(S,"Now a second program. Relation A B C with A B gives C, and C gives B. The code finds the keys A B and A C."),
(N,"Twist four. C gives B, but C is not a key, so the relation breaks B C N F. Yet B is part of a key, so it passes third normal form. Third normal form is looser than B C N F.")],
"idx":[(N,"Vault five. Indexing."),
(R,"Why does an index make queries fast?"),
(S,"Think of the index at the back of a book. Without it you read every page. With it you jump to the page. In a database, a full scan costs n. A sorted index with binary search costs log of n."),
(S,"The code builds a table of a thousand rows and asks for x equals seven. Before the index, the plan says scan. After creating the index, the plan says search using index. Same query, different path."),
(S,"Real databases use B plus trees. Each node holds many keys, the fan out. With fan out one hundred, a million rows need a tree of height three. Three disk reads instead of a million."),
(R,"And hashing?"),
(S,"Hash indexes jump straight to a bucket, constant time, but only for equality, never for ranges. B plus trees keep the leaves linked and sorted, so ranges are easy."),
(S,"A clustered index decides how the rows are stored on disk, so there is only one. A non clustered index is a separate sorted list pointing to the rows."),
(N,"Twist five. An index on a column with only a few values, like gender, helps little. And a search like name like percent son cannot use the index at all, because the start is unknown.")],
"pre":[(N,"Vault six. Data preprocessing."),
(R,"Why clean data before learning from it?"),
(S,"Because garbage in, garbage out. Real data has missing values, outliers and different scales. You can drop the row, fill with the mean or median, or predict the missing value."),
(S,"Scaling puts columns on one scale. Min max scaling maps to zero to one. Z score subtracts the mean and divides by the standard deviation. Look at the code with ten, twenty, thirty, forty, one hundred."),
(S,"Min max gives zero, point one one one, point two two two, point three three three, one. Z score gives minus point nine four nine up to one point eight nine seven."),
(R,"Anything else?"),
(S,"Binning turns numbers into ranges. Equal width bins use the same size. Equal depth bins hold the same count. Smoothing replaces values by the bin mean. Also remove duplicates and fix inconsistent labels."),
(N,"Twist six. One outlier, a hundred among small numbers, squeezes everything else into the bottom of the min max scale. Z score handles it better, and a median handles it best.")],
"dw":[(N,"Vault seven. Data warehouse modelling."),
(R,"We already have a database. Why build a warehouse?"),
(S,"A database serves daily work, O L T P, many small updates. A warehouse serves questions, O L A P, big read only summaries over years of history, collected from many sources."),
(S,"The model is a star schema. In the middle is a fact table with the numbers, like sales amount, and foreign keys. Around it are dimension tables, like time, product and store. A snowflake schema splits dimensions into smaller tables."),
(R,"What do analysts do with it?"),
(S,"Roll up, going to a coarser level, day to month. Drill down, the reverse. Slice, fix one dimension. Dice, pick a sub cube. Pivot, rotate the view."),
(S,"A data cube holds every group by. The code lists them for three dimensions and counts eight."),
(N,"Twist seven. n dimensions give two to the n group bys. Add one more dimension and the cube doubles. That is why warehouses precompute only the useful ones.")],
"dsa":[(N,"Lab. The complexity of joining tables."),
(R,"A join looks simple. Why does cost matter?"),
(S,"Because the naive way compares every row of R with every row of S. That is nested loops, n times m. With a thousand rows and a hundred rows, a hundred thousand comparisons."),
(S,"A hash join is smarter. Build a dictionary from the small table, then probe it once per row of the big one. The cost is n plus m, not n times m."),
(N,"The code ran both. Nested loops did one hundred thousand comparisons. The hash join did eleven hundred. Both returned the same one thousand rows."),
(R,"And attribute closure, how slow is that?"),
(S,"Each pass checks every dependency, and there can be as many passes as attributes. So polynomial, quadratic in the worst case, and fast enough for the exam."),
(N,"D S A twist. The dictionary is a hash table, the same structure behind a hash index. One data structure serves joins, indexes and deduplication.")],
"wk":[(N,"Walkthrough. Two algorithms from this film, line by line."),
(S,"First, attribute closure. The dependencies are A gives B, and B gives C. The result starts as just A, the attributes we know."),
(S,"Go through the rules. The left side A is already known, and B is new, so add B. The next rule, B gives C, comes after, so on this pass it is checked with B already known, and C is added."),
(S,"Changed is true, so we loop once more. This time no rule adds anything, changed stays false, and the loop ends. The closure is A, B, C."),
(N,"Main logic. Keep adding what the known attributes determine, until nothing new appears."),
(S,"Now the hash join. R has three rows with join keys two, three, two. S has two rows, key two with x, key three with y."),
(S,"Build step. Put every row of S into a dictionary, key to value. Two maps to x, three maps to y."),
(S,"Probe step. For each row of R, look its key up in the dictionary. Row one finds x, row two finds y, row three finds x again."),
(N,"Main logic. Pay once to build the dictionary, then every lookup is instant. That turns n times m into n plus m.")],
"end":[(N,"The vault is open. E R models, S Q L, constraints, normal forms, indexes, preprocessing and the warehouse."),
(R,"So a database is rules plus speed, and a warehouse is a database built for questions."),
(S,"Next, machine learning. See you there.")],
}
